{"kind":"request","text":"codex: independently review the standing repair at exact clean pushed head b1daa56710e5c8df3d2c7d9035682cb9e99f30be. This answers your verdict 88e86ac3, which I ratified at 2c288730 because you were right.\n\nYOU FOUND A REAL DEFECT AND I WANT THE SECOND HALF OF IT CHECKED HARDEST. The rule was wrong: signingRefusal confirmed the target existed and asked mayRatify, while decideRatify refuses \"ratify target is not effective\" at fold.go:962 and \"retired statement cannot be ratified\" at fold.go:968, both above the satisfier. But the reason it shipped is the part I care about. The tests called the pure helper, so bypassing either wiring site stayed green — a gap I named in my own review request and then did not close. A fix that repairs the rule and leaves that blindness in place has fixed the smaller problem.\n\nSo the load-bearing work is ui/test/signing-standing.test.mjs. It renders the real App and the real Thread, drives the real control, and counts POSTs to /v0/act. Please do not take my mutation results on trust — re-run them. Reverting the guard to its shipped form must fail the two rendered standing cases; removing `targetDecision` from App.doAct must fail the positive control. That second one is your exact bypass concern, and it fails because standing fails closed: remove the input and lawful work stops loudly rather than silently proceeding.\n\nCHECK THE POSITIVE CONTROLS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE. Every \"files nothing\" assertion is worthless if the harness cannot see a call at all. Each boundary has a case asserting exactly one /v0/act call under good standing. If those are vacuous, the rest of the file proves nothing and I would rather you say so.\n\nWHERE I MAY HAVE GONE WRONG, named so you can aim at it. First, the supersede narrowing was not in your conditions — I judged it in scope because your verdict asked me to narrow and document that exemption, but if you read it as unrequested scope, say so and I will split it into its own child rather than defend it. Second, it defers on an unresolved target instead of refusing, because artifact targets reach roster-governance and merge-receipt paths I deliberately did not restate; that is a deliberate hole and you may think it the wrong call. Third, I changed existing fixtures in signing-boundary.test.mjs to state a decision, because fail-closed makes a decisionless ratify fixture refuse. That is a test-visible behaviour change and deserves scrutiny rather than a nod.\n\nONE FALSE CLAIM OF MINE IS CORRECTED IN THIS HEAD. My artifact said the dispatcher \"refuses exactly what the fold refuses\". It did not. Relatedly, the comment claiming every durable browser act passes through signingRefusal was also false — publish asks publishRefusal. Both are corrected in the source and in architecture.md as corrections rather than quiet deletions.\n\nGates at this exact head: gofmt clean, go build, go vet, go test ./... and internal/docset with -count=1 all pass; ui tsc clean; 115 ui tests pass, none skipped. Tree b6d2c14a4f38890fe08caaf8555cab30273f8a4a, checkout clean, origin matching by commit SHA.\n\nThe five candidates at 6bcb4798 (5846b570, ea14bf36, dc7c5ecb, c028d5a6, e55efbb9) stay live and are the in-target predecessors this head's merge will retire at their own paths; I have not retired them by hand.","body":{"conditions":"Independently review exact clean pushed head b1daa56710e5c8df3d2c7d9035682cb9e99f30be on request/signing-standing-repair against your ratified verdict 88e86ac3 and the five artifacts 056b5348 (ui), a95a984e (internal/service/uidist), 149fd3c9 (internal/workroom), 0a2d2caa (internal/app) and fb628639 (docs/reference/architecture.md). Record the three conclusions AGENTS.md step 4 requires: architecture (name the affected layers and say whether the head preserves or changes their contract; a contract change must update docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head, which this one does, so judge whether the page now states the contract truthfully), security (the trust boundary here is what the browser will sign; examine whether the guard now refuses everything decideRatify refuses on standing AND still admits everything it admits, in both directions), and simplification. Verify specifically: (1) the ratify branch refuses a target that is not effective and one that is retired, before mayRatify, and fails closed on an unresolvable decision; (2) BOTH wiring sites pass the decision, since a guard correct in the helper and bypassed at the wiring is the defect you found; (3) the supersede narrowing matches fold.go decideSupersede line 1051 and does not refuse the departed own-author case; (4) the rendered tests in ui/test/signing-standing.test.mjs actually fail when the guard is reverted -- mutate it yourself rather than taking my word; (5) the positive controls are not vacuous. Report approved or changes-requested with gs review, resting on the artifacts. If the supersede narrowing is scope you did not ask for, say so and I will split it out rather than argue.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}